window.CJ_MAP = [{"id":"hypothyroid","sys":"Hormones & glucose","name":"Hypothyroidism and myxedema coma","patient":"A 72-year-old says she has been tired and cold for months, is constipated and has gained weight without eating more.","topics":["55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","55-myxedema-coma"],"report":["The client with hypothyroidism is increasingly lethargic and difficult to arouse; RR 10 and shallow, temperature 94.8°F (34.9°C), HR 48, BP 88/50.","Hypothyroidism on levothyroxine; family reports missed doses this week. Received an opioid for pain 3 hours ago.","Hypoventilation, hypothermia, bradycardia and hypotension with decreasing LOC — I am concerned about myxedema coma.","Please evaluate now. I have positioned the airway and applied oxygen per protocol; requesting a glucose and sodium level and orders for IV thyroid hormone."],"nodes":[{"id":"hypothyroid","name":"Hypothyroidism & myxedema coma"}],"drugs":[{"id":"levothyroxine","name":"Levothyroxine"}],"videos":[{"id":"EIFmHupBNi0","title":"Hypothyroidism & myxedema coma (Level Up RN)","see":"Primary vs secondary causes, the slowed-down signs and labs, and the emergency of myxedema coma"}],"steps":[{"k":"rc","gist":"Signs of a <b>hypometabolic state</b> that develop <b>gradually</b>: cold intolerance, fatigue, constipation, weight gain, <b>bradycardia</b>, <b>hypoventilation</b>, and <b>myxedema</b> (periorbital edema, macroglossia, hoarseness, nonpitting edema). Compare with the client’s <b>baseline a year ago</b>.","terms":[{"t":"Cold intolerance and hypothermia","see":"Wears a sweater in warm weather; temperature below 97°F (36.1°C).","why":"Thyroid hormone (T3) drives heat production: it increases the number of <b>Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumps</b> and mitochondrial activity in nearly every cell, and running them burns fuel and releases heat. With too little T3, cells burn less fuel, so the body makes less heat (<b>decreased thermogenesis</b>).","means":"A direct reading of the metabolic rate. A temperature that keeps falling — especially with a falling level of consciousness — points toward <b>myxedema coma</b>."},{"t":"Fatigue, lethargy and somnolence","see":"Sleeps more, tires with ordinary activity, slow to respond.","why":"Lower T3 means <b>less cellular energy turnover</b> in muscle and brain, and slower nerve and muscle function.","means":"Gradual, so ask about activity <b>compared with a year ago</b>. Increasing somnolence is a red flag for myxedema coma."},{"t":"Bradycardia (HR &lt; 60) and decreased cardiac output","see":"Slow pulse, low activity tolerance, dyspnea on exertion.","why":"T3 normally raises heart rate and contractility (in part by increasing <b>beta-adrenergic receptors</b> in the heart). Without it the heart beats slower and weaker, and the myocardium becomes <b>flabby</b> with enlarged chambers, so <b>cardiac output falls</b>.","means":"This is why <b>decreased perfusion</b> is a priority problem — and why replacement starts low: suddenly raising metabolic demand on this heart can cause <b>angina, dysrhythmias or MI</b>."},{"t":"Hypoventilation","see":"Slow, shallow respirations; SpO₂ may drop.","why":"Low thyroid hormone <b>blunts the brain’s drive to breathe</b> in response to low oxygen and high CO₂, and weakens the respiratory muscles, so CO₂ builds up.","means":"The <b>top priority</b> (impaired gas exchange). <b>Sedatives and opioids</b> depress breathing further — avoid them or expect a reduced dose, and monitor RR and SpO₂."},{"t":"Myxedema: periorbital edema and nonpitting edema","see":"Puffy face and eyelids; firm swelling of the hands, feet and shins that does not keep a dent.","why":"<b>Glycosaminoglycans</b> (water-attracting protein–sugar molecules such as hyaluronic acid) build up in the skin and tissues and <b>hold water in a gel</b>. The water is bound in that matrix rather than loose in the tissue spaces, so pressing does not push it away — the edema is <b>nonpitting</b>.","means":"Distinguishes myxedema from heart-failure or kidney edema, which <b>pits</b>. It also explains the thick tongue and the hoarse voice."},{"t":"Macroglossia and hoarseness","see":"Enlarged, thick tongue; slow or slurred speech; deep, hoarse voice.","why":"The same <b>glycosaminoglycan deposits</b> enlarge the tongue and thicken the <b>vocal cords</b>.","means":"An <b>airway</b> consideration — a large tongue can obstruct the airway in a sedated or obtunded client. Slurred speech here is from the tongue, not necessarily a stroke, but assess neurologic status."},{"t":"Constipation","see":"Fewer, harder stools.","why":"Low T3 slows <b>GI smooth-muscle activity</b> (decreased peristalsis, or <b>hypomotility</b>).","means":"Also a dosing clue on treatment: constipation and more sleep suggest <b>underreplacement</b>."},{"t":"Weight gain without overeating","see":"Weight up compared with a year ago despite a normal or lower appetite.","why":"A lower <b>basal metabolic rate</b> burns fewer calories at rest, and myxedema adds retained water.","means":"Weigh and compare with the past year; weight should stabilize on replacement."},{"t":"Dry, coarse, cool, pale skin and hair loss","see":"Dry scaly skin, brittle hair, loss of the outer eyebrows, poor wound healing.","why":"Less <b>sweat and sebaceous secretion</b>; <b>skin vessels constrict</b> to conserve heat (cool, pale); slower cell turnover and protein synthesis slow hair growth and <b>wound healing</b>.","means":"Protect skin integrity; expect slower healing after injury or surgery."},{"t":"Decreased deep tendon reflexes","see":"Sluggish reflexes, with a slow relaxation phase.","why":"Muscle fibers <b>contract and relax more slowly</b> when their metabolic rate is low.","means":"A classic physical-exam sign of hypothyroidism."},{"t":"Cognitive slowing, apathy and depression","see":"Slow answers, poor memory and attention, withdrawn; the family often notices first.","why":"Thyroid hormone supports <b>brain metabolism and neurotransmission</b>; when it is low, thinking and mood slow.","means":"Can look like dementia or depression in older adults. A <b>worsening mental status</b> is the earliest warning of myxedema coma — assess it at least every 8 hours."},{"t":"Myxedema coma: hyponatremia, hypoglycemia, hypotension","see":"Falling LOC with low sodium, low glucose and low BP.","why":"Severe hormone deficiency impairs the kidneys’ ability to excrete free water (<b>dilutional hyponatremia</b>), slows <b>glucose production</b> by the liver, and lowers cardiac output (<b>hypotension</b>).","means":"Each one is treated in the emergency box: saline, IV glucose, IV thyroid hormone, airway. <b>Do not wait for labs</b> to act."}],"cards":[{"t":"Ask","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"activity now <b>compared with a year ago</b> (changes are slow and easily missed, especially in older adults); more <b>sleep</b>; <b>fatigue</b>, weakness, muscle aches, <b>paresthesias</b>; <b>constipation</b>; <b>cold intolerance</b> (extra blankets or a sweater in warm weather); low libido, heavy prolonged periods or amenorrhea, trouble conceiving (anovulation), impotence and infertility in men; <b>lithium or amiodarone</b>; past hyperthyroid treatment and which one; <b>COVID-19</b> (can aggravate autoimmune thyroid disease).","bullets":["Activity vs <b>a year ago</b> (slow change, often missed in older adults), ↑ sleep, <b>fatigue</b>, aches, <b>paresthesias</b>","<b>Constipation</b>, <b>cold intolerance</b> (sweater in warm weather)","Libido, heavy periods/amenorrhea, anovulation, impotence + infertility (men)","<b>Lithium, amiodarone</b>, past hyperthyroid treatment (which one), <b>COVID-19</b>"],"why":"Every finding is the same idea — <b>not enough fuel burning</b>: less heat (cold intolerance, low temperature), less drive (slow pulse, slow breathing, slow gut, slow thinking), and tissues that collect water-holding compounds (puffy face, thick tongue). The danger is in the breathing: slow, shallow respiration plus any sedative can tip into respiratory failure."},{"t":"See","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"<b>coarse dry scaly skin</b> (cool, pale or ash gray) with <b>poor wound healing</b>; dry, coarse, brittle hair that grows less, <b>loss of eyebrow hair</b>, thick brittle nails; <b>puffy face and periorbital edema</b>, <b>thick tongue</b>, <b>hoarseness</b>, <b>nonpitting edema of the hands and feet</b>; slow movement, <b>slow or slurred speech</b> (the thick tongue) and slow answers; <b>weight gain</b> without overeating (weigh and compare with a year ago); possibly a <b>goiter</b>.","bullets":["<b>Coarse dry cool skin</b>, <b>poor wound healing</b>; dry coarse brittle hair, <b>lost eyebrows</b>, thick nails","<b>Puffy face + eyes</b>, <b>thick tongue</b>, <b>hoarse</b>, <b>nonpitting edema of hands + feet</b>","Slow movement, <b>slow or slurred speech</b>, slow answers","<b>Weight gain</b> without overeating (weigh, compare with a year ago) · maybe <b>goiter</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Measure","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"pulse <b>below 60</b>, slow respirations (<b>hypoventilation</b>), temperature <b>below 97°F (36.1°C)</b>, <b>diastolic hypertension</b>, decreased tendon reflexes, <b>hearing loss</b>, decreased activity tolerance, dyspnea, pericardial or pleural effusion, abdominal distention or ascites.","bullets":["HR <b>&lt; 60</b>, slow RR (<b>hypoventilation</b>), temp <b>&lt; 97°F (36.1°C)</b>","<b>Diastolic hypertension</b>, ↓ reflexes, <b>hearing loss</b>, ↓ activity tolerance, dyspnea, effusions, distention/ascites"],"why":""},{"t":"Mind and mood","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"<b>apathy and depression</b> — sometimes the family brings the patient in; lethargy may keep them from noticing they have changed. <b>Confusion</b>, lethargy or somnolence, inattentiveness; impaired <b>attention and memory</b> and slowed thinking — families describe the patient as <b>withdrawn</b> → <b>social isolation</b>.","bullets":["<b>Apathy, depression</b> (family often notices first)","<b>Confusion</b>, somnolence, inattentive; ↓ attention + memory, slow thinking","Withdrawn → <b>isolation</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Myxedema coma — what you see","tag":"If it worsens","topic":"55-myxedema-coma","text":"greatly <b>reduced consciousness and cognition</b>, <b>respiratory failure</b>, <b>hypotension</b>, <b>hyponatremia</b>, <b>hypothermia</b>, <b>hypoglycemia</b>, <b>bradycardia</b>.","bullets":["↓ <b>LOC + cognition</b>, <b>respiratory failure</b>","<b>Hypotension, hyponatremia, hypothermia, hypoglycemia, bradycardia</b> (everything low)"],"why":"Read the list of findings as \"everything that depends on metabolism has failed\": no heat (hypothermia), no drive to breathe (respiratory failure), no pump (bradycardia, hypotension), no fuel (hypoglycemia), and dilutional low sodium. Each line of the emergency box answers one of them — and warming is with <b>blankets</b>, not aggressive active rewarming, while the hormone is replaced <b>IV</b> because the slowed gut will not absorb a pill."}]},{"k":"ac","gist":"Deficient T3/T4 lowers the <b>basal metabolic rate</b> in every tissue. <b>Elevated TSH with low free T4</b> = <b>primary hypothyroidism</b>: the gland fails and the pituitary keeps signaling — most often <b>Hashimoto thyroiditis</b>.","terms":[{"t":"TSH elevated, free T4 low","see":"TSH above 5 μU/mL with low T3/T4.","why":"<b>Negative feedback</b>: the pituitary releases TSH until it senses enough thyroid hormone. When the gland cannot respond, hormone stays low and the pituitary keeps raising TSH.","means":"<b>Primary hypothyroidism</b> (the problem is in the thyroid). TSH low or normal with low T4 points to a <b>secondary</b> (pituitary or hypothalamic) cause. On treatment, TSH guides the dose."},{"t":"Goiter","see":"Enlarged thyroid at the front of the neck.","why":"Persistently high <b>TSH stimulates thyroid cells to grow</b> even though they cannot make more hormone (for example, with iodine deficiency or Hashimoto).","means":"A goiter says “thyroid problem,” <b>not which direction</b> — Graves disease also causes one."},{"t":"Hashimoto thyroiditis","see":"The most common cause in the US.","why":"An <b>autoimmune</b> attack destroys thyroid tissue, so the gland gradually loses the ability to make hormone.","means":"Permanent → <b>lifelong replacement</b>. Other causes to ask about: thyroid surgery, radioactive iodine, <b>amiodarone, lithium</b>."}],"cards":[{"t":"Definition","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"reduced or absent thyroid hormone secretion → <b>whole-body slowing of metabolism</b>. Early on it is easily missed: onset is <b>gradual</b> and symptoms mimic many other diseases.","bullets":["↓ or absent thyroid hormone → <b>everything slows</b>","<b>Gradual onset</b>, mimics other diseases → easily missed"],"why":""},{"t":"Causes (Box 55.1)","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"most US cases are <b>Hashimoto thyroiditis</b>, an autoimmune attack on thyroid tissue. Also common in North America: <b>thyroid surgery</b> and <b>radioactive iodine</b> treatment of hyperthyroidism. Worldwide: <b>iodine-poor soil and water</b> (endemic goiter) — the gland needs dietary <b>iodine and tyrosine</b> to make hormone. Drugs: <b>amiodarone, lithium</b>. Cancer (thyroid or metastatic), congenital problems. All of these are <b>primary</b> (the gland itself fails). <b>Secondary</b>: not enough TSH from congenital pituitary defects or pituitary or hypothalamic tumors, trauma, infection or infarction.","bullets":["US: <b>Hashimoto</b> (autoimmune) most common","Also: <b>thyroid surgery</b>, <b>radioactive iodine</b>","Worldwide: <b>iodine deficiency</b> (endemic goiter); hormone needs <b>iodine + tyrosine</b>","Drugs: <b>amiodarone, lithium</b> · cancer (thyroid or metastatic) · congenital → all <b>primary</b> (gland fails)","<b>Secondary</b>: pituitary/hypothalamus can't make TSH (congenital defects, tumors, trauma, infection, infarction)"],"why":""},{"t":"Labs (Table 55.1)","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"<b>T3 and T4 low</b>. <b>TSH high in primary</b> hypothyroidism (the gland fails, the pituitary shouts), <b>low or near normal in secondary</b> (the pituitary or hypothalamus fails). Older adults tend to run higher TSH.","bullets":["<b>T3 + T4 low</b>","<b>TSH high = primary</b> (gland fails) · <b>TSH low/normal = secondary</b> (pituitary/hypothalamus)"],"why":""},{"t":"Who","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"<b>4–10% of adults</b>; women <b>5 to 8 times</b> more often than men. A congenital form is genetic, and thyroid dysfunction is linked to Down syndrome, Turner syndrome and atrial fibrillation. There is <b>no known prevention</b> — catch it early.","bullets":["<b>4–10%</b> of adults; <b>women 5–8×</b> more","No known prevention → <b>watch for symptoms early</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Older adults","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"secretion falls with age but so does clearance, so <b>don't rely on labs alone</b> — use <b>changes in symptoms</b> to judge replacement (symptoms are often milder at this age). Muscle loss is normal aging, so don't use strength as a sign. Age-related heart and nerve changes make them <b>more sensitive to replacement</b> — <b>start low, increase slowly</b> to avoid cardiac and neurologic toxicity, and assess for <b>chest pain, shortness of breath</b> and mental status changes (<b>confusion</b>, changes in sleep). Amiodarone can change thyroid levels.","bullets":["Labs less reliable (↓ secretion + ↓ clearance) → judge replacement by <b>symptom changes</b>; milder symptoms","Don't use muscle strength as a sign","<b>More sensitive to replacement</b> (heart + nerves) → <b>start low, go slow</b>","Assess <b>chest pain, dyspnea, confusion, sleep changes</b> · amiodarone alters levels"],"why":""}]},{"k":"ph","gist":"1. <b>Impaired gas exchange</b> from hypoventilation. 2. <b>Decreased cardiac output</b> and perfusion. 3. Risk for <b>myxedema coma</b>, which is fatal mostly through <b>respiratory failure</b>.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Priority problems","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"1. <b>Decreased gas exchange</b> from low energy, muscle weakness and fatigue. 2. <b>Reduced perfusion</b> from low cardiac output. 3. Potential for <b>myxedema coma</b>. Respiratory failure is the most common cause of death in myxedema coma.","bullets":["1. <b>↓ Gas exchange</b> · 2. <b>↓ Perfusion</b> (low CO) · 3. <b>Myxedema coma</b> risk","Myxedema coma deaths: mostly <b>respiratory failure</b>"],"why":"Replacement has to be slow because the heart has adapted to a low metabolic demand. Thyroid hormone raises <b>oxygen demand</b> in every tissue, including the myocardium — give a full dose to a 70-year-old with coronary disease and you have created the conditions for angina, dysrhythmia and infarction. That is also why the empty-stomach rule matters: absorption varies with food and minerals, and a dose that is absorbed unevenly cannot be titrated."},{"t":"Myxedema coma — what it is","tag":"","topic":"55-myxedema-coma","text":"a rare but life-threatening complication of <b>untreated or poorly treated hypothyroidism</b>: dangerously reduced <b>cardiopulmonary and neurologic function</b> (few patients are actually comatose). Mortality is extremely high — early recognition is everything. Low cardiac output starves the brain and other organs of blood and oxygen; <b>respiratory failure</b> is the most common cause of death.","bullets":["Untreated/poorly treated hypothyroidism → <b>heart, lungs, brain fail</b>","Few are truly comatose; <b>mortality very high</b> → recognize early","Low cardiac output → organ failure; <b>respiratory failure</b> = most common cause of death"],"why":""},{"t":"Myxedema coma — triggers","tag":"","topic":"55-myxedema-coma","text":"any hypothyroid patient who is <b>newly diagnosed</b> or has other health problems is at risk. Triggers: <b>acute illness, surgery, chemotherapy, stopping thyroid replacement, sedatives or opioids</b>.","bullets":["At risk: <b>newly diagnosed</b> or other illness","Triggers: <b>acute illness, surgery, chemo, stopping replacement, sedatives/opioids</b>"],"why":""}]},{"k":"gs","gist":"Maintain <b>ventilation and perfusion</b>; start <b>levothyroxine at a low dose and titrate slowly</b> by TSH and symptoms; teach <b>lifelong</b> replacement with self-monitoring.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Levothyroxine — how to give it","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"lifelong synthetic thyroid hormone. Start at a set dose and raise gradually over weeks; the <b>lowest starting dose</b> for severe symptoms, older adults, coronary disease or unknown severity — too much or too fast causes <b>dysrhythmias, heart failure and MI</b>. Final dose is set by <b>TSH</b> and how the patient feels. <b>IV</b> in myxedema coma (the gut absorbs poorly).","bullets":["<b>Lifelong</b>; raise gradually over weeks","<b>Lowest start</b>: severe symptoms, older adults, <b>coronary disease</b>","Too much/too fast → <b>dysrhythmias, HF, MI</b>","Dose by <b>TSH</b> + symptoms · <b>IV in myxedema coma</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Self-monitoring and teaching","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"the two easiest checks are <b>sleep and bowels</b>: needing more sleep and <b>constipated</b> → dose may be too low; <b>trouble falling asleep and more bowel movements</b> → dose may be too high. Teach lifelong therapy, signs of hypo- and hyperthyroidism, <b>medical alert bracelet</b>, periodic blood tests, <b>no OTC drugs without asking</b>, a balanced diet with <b>fiber and fluids</b>, warmth until symptoms improve, rest. One person should be responsible for preparing doses so none are <b>missed or doubled</b>; one-floor living may help for a while. A patient on replacement who is <b>still tired and cold</b> (a sweater in warm weather) shows signs of too little hormone: take <b>vital signs and temperature</b>, <b>review the medication record</b> (dose, missed doses, how and when it is taken) and report — the provider may check the TSH and adjust the dose. Make sure whoever oversees daily care (family, facility staff) knows about the condition; arrange home health if needed.","bullets":["Check <b>sleep + bowels</b>: more sleep + <b>constipation</b> = too low; insomnia + more stools = <b>too high</b>","Lifelong · <b>medical alert</b> · blood tests · <b>no OTC without asking</b>","Fiber + fluids, warmth, rest · one person manages doses (none missed or doubled) · one-floor living","On replacement but <b>still tired + cold</b> → <b>VS + temperature</b>, <b>review med record</b> (dose, missed doses, timing) → report (TSH, dose change)","Caregivers/facility know the diagnosis · home health if needed"],"why":""}]},{"k":"ta","gist":"Monitor <b>RR, depth and SpO₂</b>; <b>avoid or reduce sedatives</b>; monitor HR, rhythm and BP; give <b>levothyroxine on an empty stomach</b> 30–60 minutes before breakfast; report <b>chest pain</b>. Myxedema coma: <b>patent airway</b>, IV saline, glucose and <b>IV thyroid hormone</b>, warm with <b>blankets</b>.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Gas exchange","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"record rate and depth, <b>SpO₂</b>, oxygen for hypoxemia, listen for decreased sounds or crackles; ventilatory support if severe. <b>Avoid sedation</b>; if needed, use <b>reduced doses</b> — hypothyroidism makes patients more sensitive — and watch gas exchange closely.","bullets":["Rate, depth, <b>SpO₂</b>, O₂ if hypoxemic, crackles; ventilator if severe","<b>Avoid sedatives</b>; if needed <b>lower doses</b> (↑ sensitivity) + watch breathing"],"why":""},{"t":"Cardiac output","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"watch for a rising BP and <b>dysrhythmias</b> — monitor <b>BP, heart rate and rhythm</b> and act on changes before complications set in; chronic disease may bring cardiovascular disease. Teach to report <b>chest pain or discomfort immediately</b>.","bullets":["BP may rise, <b>dysrhythmias</b> → monitor <b>BP, HR, rhythm</b>","Chronic → cardiovascular disease · report <b>chest pain</b> at once"],"why":""},{"t":"Levothyroxine — drug alert","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"take it exactly as prescribed, never change the dose alone. <b>With water, on an empty stomach, 30–60 minutes before breakfast.</b> Keep <b>iron, calcium and antacids at least 4 hours apart</b>; fiber supplements can also block absorption. Watch for <b>chest pain and dyspnea</b> when starting, and teach the signs of <b>too much</b> — <b>fast or pounding heartbeat, chest pain, nervousness or tremor, trouble sleeping, feeling hot and sweaty, weight loss, more frequent stools</b>.","bullets":["<b>Water, empty stomach, 30–60 min before breakfast</b>","<b>Iron, calcium, antacids ≥ 4 h apart</b>; fiber supplements interfere","Watch <b>chest pain, dyspnea</b> at start; teach signs of <b>too much</b>: fast heartbeat, chest pain, tremor, <b>insomnia</b>, heat intolerance, weight loss, <b>more stools</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Myxedema coma — action alert","tag":"If it worsens","topic":"55-myxedema-coma","text":"it can progress to <b>shock, organ damage and death</b>, so assess every hypothyroid patient at least <b>every 8 hours</b>, especially <b>mental status</b>, and report changes promptly. Treatment starts on the symptoms — <b>do not wait for lab confirmation</b>.","bullets":["Can → <b>shock, organ damage, death</b>","Assess hypothyroid patients <b>at least every 8 h</b> (mental status!)","Treat on symptoms — <b>don't wait for labs</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Myxedema coma — emergency care","tag":"If it worsens","topic":"55-myxedema-coma","text":"keep a <b>patent airway</b>; IV <b>normal or hypertonic saline</b>; <b>IV T3 and/or T4</b>; <b>IV glucose</b>; <b>corticosteroids</b>; temperature and <b>BP hourly</b>; <b>warm blankets</b>; watch mental status; <b>turn every 2 hours</b>; <b>aspiration precautions</b>.","bullets":["<b>Airway</b> first","IV <b>normal/hypertonic saline</b>, <b>IV T3/T4</b>, <b>IV glucose</b>, <b>steroids</b>","Temp + <b>BP hourly</b>, <b>warm blankets</b>, mental status","<b>Turn q2h</b>, <b>aspiration precautions</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Myxedema coma — IV hormone and drug cautions","tag":"If it worsens","topic":"55-myxedema-coma","text":"thyroid hormone goes <b>IV</b> because the slowed gut absorbs pills poorly, but it carries the same risk as any start of replacement: too much too fast → <b>dysrhythmias, heart failure or MI</b> — watch rhythm, BP, chest pain and dyspnea, especially in <b>older or cardiac</b> patients. <b>Sedatives and opioids</b> both trigger and deepen the coma: avoid them; if one is essential, expect a <b>reduced dose</b> and monitor breathing and SpO₂.","bullets":["<b>IV</b> T3/T4 (gut won't absorb) — same cardiac risk as any start: <b>dysrhythmias, HF, MI</b>","Watch rhythm, BP, chest pain, dyspnea — esp. <b>older/cardiac</b>","<b>Avoid sedatives/opioids</b>; if essential → <b>reduced dose</b> + watch breathing, SpO₂"],"why":""}]},{"k":"eo","gist":"<b>HR above 60</b>, <b>SpO₂ above 93%</b>, improving cognition. Dose check: more sleep and constipation suggest <b>underreplacement</b>; insomnia, palpitations and more frequent stools suggest <b>overreplacement</b>.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Evaluate","tag":"","topic":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","text":"pulse <b>above 60</b> and BP normal for the patient; <b>SpO₂ above 93%</b>; improving cognition.","bullets":["Pulse <b>&gt; 60</b>, BP normal · <b>SpO₂ &gt; 93%</b> · cognition improving"],"why":""}]}]},{"id":"hypoglycemia","sys":"Hormones & glucose","name":"Hypoglycemia","patient":"A client on insulin is shaky, sweaty and slow to answer an hour after a late lunch tray never came.","topics":["56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule"],"report":["The client’s blood glucose is 48 mg/dL; diaphoretic, confused and unable to swallow safely.","Type 2 diabetes on insulin glargine and lispro; received lispro at noon and the lunch tray was delayed.","Severe (level 3) hypoglycemia — the client needs assistance and cannot take anything by mouth. I gave glucagon per protocol and positioned the client on the side; recheck due in 15 minutes.","Requesting an order for IV dextrose if it stays low, and a review of the insulin doses."],"nodes":[{"id":"dm-acute","name":"Lows, DKA & HHS"}],"drugs":[{"id":"glucagon","name":"Glucagon"},{"id":"d50","name":"Dextrose 50% (D50)"}],"videos":[{"id":"OoiX6jx48L8","title":"The 15:15 rule (Dish on Diabetes)","see":"15 g of fast carbohydrate, recheck in 15 minutes, repeat until the glucose is back in range"},{"id":"ZUlx6F63gj0","title":"How to use glucagon (Mayo Clinic)","see":"The rescue for a person too low to swallow safely — what is in the kit and how it is given"},{"id":"NXd0uSeIV6c","title":"Diabetes complications: hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS (Level Up RN)","see":"How to tell the three apart — onset, labs, ketones — and treatment, including potassium"}],"steps":[{"k":"rc","gist":"Blood glucose <b>below 70 mg/dL</b> with <b>adrenergic (neurogenic) signs</b> — tremor, palpitations, anxiety, diaphoresis, hunger — progressing to <b>neuroglycopenic signs</b>: confusion, behavior change, seizures, loss of consciousness.","terms":[{"t":"Blood glucose below 70 mg/dL","see":"Fingerstick or lab glucose under 70; under 54 is clinically significant (level 2).","why":"Normally insulin release falls at about 83 mg/dL and glucagon and epinephrine rise at about 67 — the body’s defense. Insulin given by injection or a sulfonylurea keeps working regardless, so glucose keeps falling.","means":"Below 70 → treat. <b>Severe</b> (level 3) is defined by <b>needing another person’s help</b>, not by a number."},{"t":"Tremor, palpitations and anxiety (adrenergic)","see":"Shaky hands, pounding or fast heart, nervousness.","why":"Falling glucose triggers a <b>sympathetic surge</b>: <b>epinephrine</b> acts on beta receptors in the heart and muscle.","means":"The <b>early warning</b>. <b>Beta blockers</b> blunt these signs, so a client on one may show few of them."},{"t":"Diaphoresis, hunger and tingling (cholinergic)","see":"Sweating, sudden hunger, tingling around the mouth or fingers.","why":"Sweat glands are sympathetic but use <b>acetylcholine</b>, which is why sweating can persist even with a beta blocker; hunger and tingling are also cholinergic signals.","means":"Sweating with cool, clammy skin is a strong clue — the opposite of the warm, dry skin of hyperglycemia."},{"t":"Cool, clammy skin","see":"Pale, cool and moist skin.","why":"<b>Epinephrine constricts skin vessels</b> while the sweat glands are active.","means":"No dehydration and <b>no ketones</b> — this is not DKA or HHS."},{"t":"Confusion and behavior change (neuroglycopenia)","see":"Difficulty thinking, slurred speech, irritability, combativeness, unusual behavior.","why":"<b>Neurons depend on a continuous glucose supply</b> and store only minutes’ worth. When glucose falls, brain cells run out of energy and stop working normally.","means":"The client may no longer be able to treat themselves or <b>swallow safely</b>, which changes the route of treatment. Can be mistaken for intoxication or a stroke — <b>check the glucose</b>."},{"t":"Seizures and loss of consciousness","see":"Unresponsive or seizing.","why":"Severe brain <b>energy failure</b>; untreated, it can cause brain damage or death.","means":"<b>Severe hypoglycemia</b>: nothing by mouth; <b>glucagon or IV dextrose</b> now, side-lying position, protect the airway."},{"t":"Hypoglycemia unawareness","see":"Low glucose with few or no warning symptoms.","why":"After years of diabetes or frequent lows, the <b>counterregulatory epinephrine response weakens</b>, so the early warning signs no longer appear.","means":"These clients test more often and use <b>fingertip checks only</b>; older adults are at higher risk."}],"cards":[{"t":"Symptoms (Box 56.10)","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"<b>neurogenic (the early warning)</b> — adrenergic: <b>shaky, pounding heart, nervous</b>; cholinergic: <b>sweaty, hungry, tingling</b>. <b>Neuroglycopenic (the brain running out)</b>: weakness, fatigue, <b>difficulty thinking, confusion, behavior change</b>, emotional instability, <b>seizures, loss of consciousness</b>, brain damage, death. Skin is <b>cool and clammy</b>; no dehydration; ketones negative.","bullets":["<b>Early (neurogenic)</b>: <b>shaky, pounding heart, anxious, sweaty, hungry, tingling</b>","<b>Late (brain)</b>: <b>confusion</b>, behavior change, <b>seizures, unconsciousness</b>, death","<b>Cool, clammy</b> skin · no dehydration · no ketones"],"why":""},{"t":"Older adults","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"slower kidney clearance of sulfonylureas and insulin, <b>weaker epinephrine and glucagon responses</b>, frequent unawareness. Family checks glucose for unsteadiness, light-headedness, poor concentration, trembling or sweating; <b>bedtime snack</b> if appetite is poor.","bullets":["↓ clearance + ↓ counter-hormones + unawareness","Family checks glucose for unsteadiness, trembling, sweating; <b>bedtime snack</b>"],"why":""}]},{"k":"ac","gist":"The <b>brain depends on a continuous glucose supply</b> and cannot store it, so hypoglycemia quickly causes neurologic dysfunction. Usual cause: <b>insulin or a sulfonylurea</b> not matched to food intake or activity.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"What happens","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"below <b>70 mg/dL</b> the body releases balancing hormones and fires the autonomic nervous system. Normally insulin falls at about <b>83</b> and glucagon and epinephrine switch on at about <b>67</b> — before symptoms. The <b>brain can't make glucose</b> and stores only minutes' worth, so low glucose quickly becomes a brain problem.","bullets":["<b>&lt; 70</b> → hormones + autonomic alarm","Normal defense: insulin ↓ at ~<b>83</b>, glucagon/epinephrine ↑ at ~<b>67</b>","<b>Brain can't make or store glucose</b> → fast brain injury"],"why":""},{"t":"Causes","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"too much insulin for the food and activity; insulin at the <b>wrong time</b> or the <b>wrong type</b>; <b>missed or delayed meals</b>; <b>gastroparesis</b>; <b>alcohol</b> (liver stops releasing glucose); <b>kidney failure</b> (insulin cleared slowly); <b>exercise</b>. At risk: everyone on <b>insulin or sulfonylureas</b> (alone or with metformin), especially older adults and those with liver or kidney impairment.","bullets":["Too much insulin, <b>wrong time/type</b>","<b>Missed/late meals</b>, <b>gastroparesis</b>, <b>alcohol</b>, <b>kidney failure</b>, <b>exercise</b>","At risk: <b>insulin + sulfonylureas</b>; older, liver/kidney disease"],"why":""}]},{"k":"ph","gist":"<b>Airway and route</b>: can the client swallow safely? Altered LOC, seizures or an unsafe swallow = <b>severe hypoglycemia</b> requiring assistance — a medical emergency.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Severity and unawareness","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"<b>mild</b>: alert and able to self-treat. <b>Severe</b>: needs <b>another person's</b> help. Long-standing diabetes can bring <b>hypoglycemic unawareness</b> — no warning symptoms — so these patients test more and <b>never use alternate sites</b>. <b>Beta blockers mask</b> the warning signs.","bullets":["<b>Mild</b> = self-treats · <b>severe</b> = needs help","<b>Unawareness</b> after years → test more, <b>fingertip only</b>","<b>Beta blockers mask</b> warnings"],"why":""}]},{"k":"gs","gist":"<b>Oral fast-acting carbohydrate</b> if alert with a safe swallow; <b>glucagon or IV dextrose</b> if not; then <b>complex carbohydrate + protein</b>, and identify the cause to prevent recurrence.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"The 15-15 rule","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"glucose <b>&lt; 70</b> or symptoms → <b>15 g of fast carbohydrate</b> (<b>30 g if &lt; 50</b>), liquid if they can swallow — <b>avoid high-potassium choices like orange juice</b> in the hospital. <b>Recheck in 15 minutes</b>; still low → <b>repeat</b>. Once above the low range → a <b>complex carbohydrate with protein</b> (crackers with nut butter) to prevent a repeat. <b>Chocolate and fatty sweets absorb slowly</b> — use glucose.","bullets":["<b>&lt; 70</b> → <b>15 g</b> fast carb (<b>30 g if &lt; 50</b>)","<b>Recheck 15 min</b> → repeat if still low","Then <b>complex carb + protein</b>","<b>No chocolate</b> (fat slows it); in hospital avoid <b>OJ</b> (potassium)"],"why":"Fast sugar works because glucose needs no digestion — it goes straight from the gut into the blood. Fat in chocolate slows stomach emptying and delays it, and complex carbohydrates have to be broken down first; that is exactly why they are used <b>after</b> the low is fixed, to keep glucose up while the fast sugar runs out."},{"t":"15 g examples (Box 56.11)","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"glucose tablets or gel · <b>½ cup (120 mL) juice or regular soda</b> · <b>5 hard candies</b> · <b>4 sugar cubes or 4 teaspoons sugar</b> · <b>1 tablespoon honey or syrup</b>. Symptoms can <b>persist after glucose is back to normal</b> — go by the 15-minute recheck, then a small complex-carbohydrate and protein snack.","bullets":["Glucose tabs/gel · <b>½ cup juice or regular soda</b> · <b>5 hard candies</b>","<b>4 tsp sugar</b> · <b>1 Tbsp honey/syrup</b>","Symptoms may <b>outlast the low</b> → go by the recheck"],"why":""}]},{"k":"ta","gist":"<b>Below 70 → 15 g</b> fast carbohydrate (<b>30 g below 50</b>), <b>recheck in 15 minutes</b>, repeat until resolved. Unable to swallow → <b>glucagon</b> (position side-lying for emesis) or <b>IV dextrose</b> per protocol.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"In the hospital","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"check glucose <b>no more than 30 minutes before</b> a meal and give rapid-acting insulin <b>just before</b> the meal; coordinate trays and insulin. Monitor before antidiabetic drugs, before meals, at bedtime and with symptoms. Inpatient lows come from the <b>wrong insulin type</b>, insulin <b>not matched to the timing of food</b>, or a <b>changed eating plan without a dose change</b>; protocols give oral carbohydrate if the patient is alert and can swallow, otherwise <b>IV dextrose or glucagon</b>.","bullets":["Check <b>≤ 30 min before</b> meals; <b>rapid insulin just before</b> eating","Check before drugs, meals, bedtime, with symptoms","Inpatient causes: <b>wrong type</b>, <b>food-timing mismatch</b>, <b>diet change without dose change</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Can't swallow — glucagon and dextrose","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"<b>glucagon</b> (the main balancing hormone) is first-line for severe lows. It can cause <b>vomiting → turn the patient on their side</b>. <b>Concentrated IV dextrose</b> (e.g., D50) is <b>hyperosmolar</b> — give carefully to avoid <b>extravasation</b> and tissue damage. Both wear off: check glucose <b>every 15 minutes until resolved</b>, then monitor for hours; target <b>70–110</b>. <b>Critical rescue</b>: glucagon → repeat in <b>10 minutes</b> if still unconscious → notify the provider.","bullets":["<b>Glucagon</b> first-line; <b>side-lying</b> (vomiting)","<b>IV dextrose</b> is hyperosmolar → <b>watch for extravasation</b>","Effects wear off → glucose <b>q15 min</b>, then for hours; target <b>70–110</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Moderate and severe at home","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"<b>moderate</b> (cold clammy pale skin, rapid pulse, rapid shallow breathing, mood change, drowsiness; usually &lt; 50): <b>30 g</b> fast carbohydrate, glucagon may be needed, recheck in 15 minutes, repeat if &lt; 60, then a snack. <b>Severe</b> (can't swallow, confused, combative, unconscious, seizing): family gives <b>glucagon IM, subcut or nasal</b>; <b>second dose in 10 minutes</b> if still unconscious; then <b>call 911</b> for IV dextrose; call the provider; a small meal once awake and not nauseated.","bullets":["<b>Moderate</b>: <b>30 g</b>, maybe glucagon, recheck 15 min","<b>Severe</b>: <b>glucagon</b> (IM/subcut/nasal) → <b>repeat in 10 min</b> → <b>911</b>","Small meal once awake and not nauseated"],"why":""}]},{"k":"eo","gist":"<b>Recheck every 15 minutes</b> until out of the low range, then <b>complex carbohydrate + protein</b>. Anticipate <b>recurrence</b> with sulfonylureas or long-acting insulin, and evaluate teaching of the 15-15 rule and glucagon.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Prevention and teaching","tag":"","topic":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","text":"personal hypoglycemia plan; <b>teach the family the 15-15 rule and how to give glucagon</b>; <b>medical alert bracelet</b>; <b>always carry glucagon, a carb source and a meter</b>; regular meal timing (<b>a meal delayed more than 30 minutes</b> raises risk on some regimens); test <b>before driving</b>; exercise when insulin isn't peaking; alcohol only with food; call the provider if lows occur <b>more than twice a week</b>.","bullets":["Family learns <b>15-15 + glucagon</b>; <b>medical alert</b>","Always carry <b>glucagon, carbs, meter</b>","<b>Meal delayed &gt; 30 min</b> → risk","Test <b>before driving</b> · lows <b>&gt; 2×/week → call provider</b>"],"why":""}]}]},{"id":"ugib","sys":"Digestion & elimination","name":"Peptic ulcer with upper GI bleeding","patient":"A client who takes ibuprofen every day for back pain vomits coffee-ground material and feels dizzy standing up.","topics":["47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies"],"report":["The client vomited about 300 mL of coffee-ground emesis; HR 118, BP 92/58, dizzy and orthostatic on standing.","Takes ibuprofen daily for chronic back pain; no known GI history.","Suspected upper GI bleed with early hypovolemia. Two large-bore IVs are in and the client is NPO.","Please evaluate now. Requesting a CBC, type and crossmatch, IV fluid orders and a GI consult for EGD."],"nodes":[{"id":"pud","name":"Gastritis & peptic ulcer"},{"id":"ugib","name":"Upper GI bleeding & gastric surgery"}],"drugs":[{"id":"pantoprazole","name":"Pantoprazole"},{"id":"omeprazole","name":"Omeprazole"},{"id":"clarithromycin","name":"Clarithromycin"},{"id":"amoxicillin","name":"Amoxicillin"}],"videos":[{"id":"V_U6czbDHLE","title":"The surprising cause of stomach ulcers (TED-Ed)","see":"How H. pylori, not stress or spicy food, was shown to cause most ulcers — and why antibiotics cure them"}],"steps":[{"k":"rc","gist":"<b>Hematemesis</b> (bright red or coffee-ground), <b>melena</b>, <b>tachycardia</b> with falling BP, <b>orthostatic hypotension</b>, and <b>new confusion</b> in an older adult — signs of <b>hypovolemia from GI blood loss</b>.","terms":[{"t":"Bright red hematemesis","see":"Vomiting fresh red blood or clots.","why":"Brisk, <b>active bleeding</b> at or above the duodenum: the blood comes up before stomach acid has time to change it.","means":"Fast blood loss — ABCs, two large-bore IVs, report <b>now</b>."},{"t":"Coffee-ground emesis","see":"Dark brown, granular vomit.","why":"Blood that <b>sat in the stomach</b> long enough for <b>gastric acid to turn hemoglobin brown</b>.","means":"A slower or older bleed — still a bleed. Report it, measure it and trend the vital signs."},{"t":"Melena","see":"Black, sticky, tarry, foul-smelling stool.","why":"Blood from the upper GI tract <b>digested on its way through the intestines</b> turns black.","means":"Upper GI source (more common with duodenal ulcers). Bright red blood from the rectum usually means a lower source or a very fast upper bleed."},{"t":"Tachycardia","see":"Pulse rising, often before the BP falls.","why":"Losing volume lowers venous return; <b>baroreceptors</b> sense the drop and trigger a <b>sympathetic response</b> that raises the heart rate to keep cardiac output up.","means":"<b>The earliest vital-sign warning</b> of hypovolemia. A rising pulse with a normal BP is compensation, not stability."},{"t":"Orthostatic hypotension and dizziness on standing","see":"BP drops and HR rises when the client sits up or stands; lightheaded, may faint.","why":"With less circulating volume, the body cannot compensate quickly when gravity pools blood in the legs.","means":"Early sign of volume loss and a <b>fall risk</b> — assist with every position change."},{"t":"Hypotension, cool clammy skin and low urine output","see":"BP falling, pale cool moist skin, urine under 30 mL/hr.","why":"Compensation is failing: the body <b>constricts skin vessels</b> to send blood to the heart and brain, and the kidneys conserve water (less perfusion, <b>ADH and aldosterone</b>).","means":"<b>Hypovolemic shock</b> — more than about 1 L lost. Fluids and blood now, report immediately."},{"t":"New confusion in an older adult","see":"Restless, disoriented, or unusually drowsy.","why":"<b>Reduced blood flow and oxygen to the brain</b>; older adults compensate less well.","means":"Can be the <b>first</b> sign of bleeding in an older adult — do not assume it is baseline."},{"t":"Burning, gnawing epigastric pain","see":"Pain between the umbilicus and the xiphoid; may wake the client at night (duodenal).","why":"<b>Acid and pepsin</b> reach the exposed tissue and nerve endings in the ulcer crater.","means":"A change from intermittent pain to <b>constant pain radiating to the back</b> suggests penetration or <b>perforation</b>."},{"t":"Rigid, board-like abdomen","see":"Sudden severe pain spreading over the abdomen; the client lies still with knees drawn up.","why":"The ulcer has <b>perforated</b> and gastric contents spill into the peritoneum (<b>chemical peritonitis</b>); the abdominal muscles spasm involuntarily to guard it.","means":"<b>Surgical emergency</b> — NPO, report immediately, watch for sepsis and shock."}],"cards":[{"t":"Hemorrhage — the most serious","tag":"","topic":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","text":"more often with <b>gastric and stress ulcers</b> and in <b>older adults</b>; recurs if <i>H. pylori</i> stays untreated or therapy lacks an H₂ blocker or PPI. Massive bleeding → <b>hematemesis</b> (bright red, or <b>coffee-ground</b> when acid has digested the blood) — bleeding at or above the duodenojejunal junction. Slow bleeding → <b>melena</b> (more common with duodenal ulcers). Box 47.2: hematemesis, melena, <b>falling Hgb/Hct and BP, rising heart rate</b>, weak pulses, <b>acute confusion in older adults</b>, vertigo, dizziness, syncope. Early detection decides the outcome: <b>monitor vital signs often</b> and report changes at once. Common sources of upper GI bleeding (Fig. 47.2): <b>esophageal varices</b>, esophageal cancer, <b>gastritis</b>, <b>gastric ulcer</b>, gastric cancer, <b>duodenal ulcer</b>.","bullets":["<b>Most serious</b>; gastric + stress ulcers, <b>older adults</b>","<b>Hematemesis</b> (red/<b>coffee-ground</b>) = upper GI","<b>Melena</b> = slow bleeding (duodenal)","↓ <b>Hgb/Hct, BP</b> · ↑ <b>HR</b> · weak pulses · <b>confusion (older)</b> · dizzy, syncope","<b>Frequent VS</b>; report changes at once (early detection)","Sources (Fig. 47.2): <b>varices</b>, esophageal + gastric <b>cancer</b>, <b>gastritis</b>, <b>gastric + duodenal ulcers</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Physical","tag":"","topic":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","text":"<b>epigastric tenderness</b> at the midline between umbilicus and xiphoid; <b>dyspepsia</b> — sharp, burning or gnawing, or pressure, fullness or hunger. Perforation: <b>rigid board-like abdomen, rebound tenderness, intense pain</b>. <b>Orthostatic BP</b> and dehydration signs for blood loss — <b>dizziness on standing</b>; older adults risk <b>falls</b>.","bullets":["<b>Epigastric</b> midline tenderness; <b>burning, gnawing</b> pain","Perforation: <b>rigid</b>, rebound","<b>Orthostatics</b>; dizziness → <b>falls</b> in older adults"],"why":""},{"t":"History","tag":"","topic":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","text":"past <i>H. pylori</i>; all drugs — <b>NSAIDs, corticosteroids, chemotherapy</b>; radiation; GI surgery (partial gastrectomy, <b>bariatric</b>); alcohol, tobacco, foods that worsen symptoms; stressors; pain pattern with eating and sleep and what relieves it. <b>A change in the pain</b> — intermittent and relieved by food or antacids becoming <b>constant and radiating to the back</b> — suggests <b>perforation</b>. Many people with active ulcers have <b>no symptoms</b>.","bullets":["<i>H. pylori</i>, <b>NSAIDs, steroids</b>, chemo, radiation, <b>bariatric surgery</b>","Pain vs meals + sleep; what helps","Pain becomes <b>constant, radiates to back</b> → <b>perforation</b>","Many have <b>no symptoms</b>"],"why":""}]},{"k":"ac","gist":"Ulceration from <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> or <b>NSAIDs</b> has eroded into a vessel. <b>Bright red</b> = brisk active bleeding; <b>coffee-ground</b> = slower bleeding exposed to gastric acid; <b>melena</b> = blood digested through the intestine.","terms":[{"t":"NSAID-induced ulcer","see":"History of daily ibuprofen, naproxen or aspirin.","why":"NSAIDs block <b>prostaglandins</b>, which normally keep the stomach lining protected (mucus, bicarbonate and blood flow). Without them, acid and pepsin erode the mucosa.","means":"Teach to <b>avoid NSAIDs</b>; misoprostol (a prostaglandin) or a PPI protects clients who must take them."},{"t":"<i>H. pylori</i> infection","see":"Positive urea breath test, stool antigen, serology or biopsy.","why":"The bacterium makes <b>urease</b>, which neutralizes acid around it, and damages the protective lining so acid can reach the tissue.","means":"Curable with antibiotics plus a PPI — the client must <b>finish the whole course</b> or the ulcer recurs."},{"t":"Hemoglobin and hematocrit falling","see":"Hgb/Hct lower than baseline, often falling between draws.","why":"Early in an acute bleed whole blood is lost, so the first value can look <b>near normal</b>; it falls as fluid shifts into the vessels and IV fluids dilute the blood.","means":"<b>Trend</b> the values and treat the vital signs — a normal first Hgb does not rule out a serious bleed."},{"t":"BUN elevated with a normal creatinine","see":"BUN high, creatinine normal.","why":"Blood in the gut is <b>digested protein</b>: it is absorbed and turned into urea by the liver.","means":"Supports an <b>upper GI</b> bleed rather than kidney failure (where creatinine also rises)."}],"cards":[{"t":"Definition and cause","tag":"","topic":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","text":"mucosal defenses fail and <b>acid and pepsin</b> erode the epithelium. Most ulcers come from <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> or <b>long-term NSAIDs</b>; other risks are the same as gastritis (alcohol, smoking, stress, caffeine). Duodenal ulcers often run in <b>families</b>. About <b>4.5 million</b> Americans a year; visits, admissions and deaths have fallen with <b>PPIs</b> and early <i>H. pylori</i> treatment.","bullets":["Defenses fail → <b>acid + pepsin</b> erode","Causes: <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> + <b>NSAIDs</b>","Duodenal: <b>family history</b>; PPIs + <i>H. pylori</i> treatment → fewer deaths"],"why":""},{"t":"Gastric vs duodenal (Table 47.2)","tag":"","topic":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","text":"<b>gastric</b>: age <b>50–70</b>; men and women equally; no blood-group link; <b>normal or low acid</b>; pain <b>30–60 minutes after a meal</b>, rarely at night, <b>worsened by eating</b>; bleeds more as <b>hematemesis</b> than melena; cancer risk raised but under 5%; heals with treatment but tends to <b>recur in the same spot</b>; the surrounding mucosa shows <b>atrophic gastritis</b>. <b>Duodenal</b>: age <b>20–50</b>; more often <b>men</b>; most often <b>blood type O</b>; <b>acid hypersecretion</b>; <b>family history</b>; pain <b>1½–3 hours after a meal</b> and often <b>wakes the patient at 1–2 a.m.</b>; <b>relieved by eating</b>; bleeds more as <b>melena</b>; no added cancer risk; remissions and flare-ups — <b>60% recur within 1 year, 90% within 2</b>; no surrounding gastritis.","bullets":["<b>Gastric</b>: <b>50–70</b>, M = F, any blood type, <b>normal/low acid</b>, <b>atrophic gastritis</b> around it","Gastric pain <b>30–60 min after meals</b>, <b>worse with food</b>, rarely at night; <b>hematemesis</b> &gt; melena","Gastric: cancer risk ↑ (&lt; 5%); heals but <b>recurs in the same spot</b>","<b>Duodenal</b>: <b>20–50</b>, <b>men</b>, <b>type O</b>, <b>hypersecretion</b>, <b>family history</b>","Duodenal pain <b>1½–3 h after meals</b> + <b>1–2 a.m.</b>, <b>food relieves</b>; <b>melena</b> &gt; hematemesis","Duodenal: no cancer risk, no surrounding gastritis; remissions + flares — <b>60% recur in 1 yr, 90% in 2</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Tests for <i>H. pylori</i>","tag":"","topic":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","text":"<b>serology</b> (antibodies) is the most common; the <b>urea breath test</b> (swallow ¹³C urea, exhale — labeled carbon means urease is present) and <b>stool antigen</b> are <b>more accurate</b>. <b>EGD is the most accurate test for PUD</b>: it shows the crater and takes biopsies (rapid urease test, cancer cytology); may be repeated every <b>4–6 weeks</b> to check healing. <b>Nuclear scan</b> (technetium) finds bleeding — no prep; a repeat scan <b>1–2 days after treatment</b> shows whether it worked. Bleeding → low <b>Hgb/Hct</b>, stool <b>occult blood</b>.","bullets":["<i>H. pylori</i>: <b>serology</b> (most common); <b>breath + stool tests</b> (more accurate)","<b>EGD</b> = most accurate for PUD; repeat q4–6 weeks","<b>Nuclear scan</b> (technetium, no prep) for bleeding; repeat <b>1–2 days</b> after treatment","Bleeding → ↓ <b>Hgb/Hct</b>, stool <b>occult blood</b>"],"why":""}]},{"k":"ph","gist":"<b>Hypovolemic shock</b> is the priority. <b>Sudden severe pain with a rigid, board-like abdomen</b> = <b>perforation</b> with peritonitis — a surgical emergency.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Perforation","tag":"","topic":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","text":"the ulcer erodes through the <b>full thickness</b> and contents spill into the peritoneum: <b>sudden, sharp mid-epigastric pain spreading over the whole abdomen</b>; <b>tender, rigid, board-like abdomen</b> (<b>peritonitis</b>), rebound tenderness; the patient lies in a <b>fetal position</b>; bowel sounds hyperactive then diminishing → <b>paralytic ileus</b>; <b>septicemia and hypovolemic shock</b> within hours. <b>A surgical emergency.</b>","bullets":["Full-thickness erosion → spill into peritoneum","<b>Sudden sharp epigastric pain → whole abdomen</b>","<b>Board-like rigid abdomen</b> (peritonitis), <b>fetal position</b>","Ileus, <b>sepsis, shock</b> within hours → <b>surgery now</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Priority problems and goals","tag":"","topic":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","text":"<b>acute or persistent pain</b> and <b>potential for upper GI bleeding</b>. Pain goal: <b>3 or less on a 0–10 scale</b>. Drug goals: <b>relieve pain, eradicate <i>H. pylori</i>, heal the ulcer, prevent recurrence</b>.","bullets":["Problems: <b>pain</b> + <b>bleeding risk</b>; pain <b>≤ 3/10</b>","Drugs: relieve pain, <b>kill <i>H. pylori</b></i>, heal, prevent recurrence"],"why":"The pain clock separates the two ulcers. A duodenal ulcer hurts when the duodenum is bathed in acid and nothing buffers it — <b>hours after a meal and in the early morning</b> — and food brings relief. A gastric ulcer sits where the food goes, so eating triggers the pain <b>soon after the meal</b> and food makes it worse."},{"t":"Pyloric (gastric outlet) obstruction","tag":"","topic":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","text":"scarring, edema or inflammation block the pylorus: <b>bloating, nausea, vomiting</b> of retained food. Persistent vomiting loses acid (H⁺ and Cl⁻) → <b>metabolic alkalosis</b> and <b>hypokalemia</b>.","bullets":["Scar/edema at <b>pylorus</b> → bloating, <b>vomiting</b>","Vomiting acid → <b>metabolic alkalosis</b> + <b>hypokalemia</b>"],"why":""}]},{"k":"gs","gist":"Restore <b>circulating volume</b> (isotonic fluids, packed RBCs), achieve <b>hemostasis</b> by endoscopy, and <b>suppress acid with a PPI</b> to stabilize the clot.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Fluids and blood","tag":"","topic":"47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies","text":"start <b>isotonic</b> fluid at once (<b>0.9% normal saline or lactated Ringer's</b>); <b>packed RBCs</b> to restore volume and Hgb/Hct; <b>fresh frozen plasma</b> if the PT is more than <b>1.5 times</b> the control. Watch I&amp;O and, in <b>older adults, fluid overload</b>; check electrolytes and <b>replace</b> what vomiting and NG suction remove (e.g., <b>K⁺</b>); trend <b>Hgb, Hct and coagulation studies</b> against baseline. Under <b>500 mL</b> loss: mild weakness and sweating. <b>Over 1 L in 24 hours</b>: <b>hypovolemic shock</b> — hypotension, chills, palpitations, diaphoresis, weak thready pulse.","bullets":["<b>NS or LR</b> now; <b>PRBCs</b>; <b>FFP</b> if PT &gt; 1.5× control","I&amp;O; <b>older adults → overload</b>","<b>Replace lytes</b> lost to vomiting/NG (K⁺); trend <b>Hgb/Hct + clotting</b> vs baseline","&lt; 500 mL: weak, sweaty · <b>&gt; 1 L/24 h → shock</b>"],"why":"<b>Coffee-ground</b> vomitus means blood has sat in the stomach long enough for acid to turn hemoglobin brown — bleeding that is slower or older. <b>Bright red</b> means fast, fresh bleeding. <b>Melena</b> is blood digested on its way through the small intestine. Reading the color tells you how fast and how far up the bleeding is."},{"t":"NG tube","tag":"","topic":"47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies","text":"a <b>large-bore NG tube</b> to: <b>find blood</b> in the stomach, <b>assess the rate</b> of bleeding, <b>prevent gastric dilation</b>, and <b>decompress</b> to rest the GI tract.","bullets":["<b>Large-bore NG</b>: detect blood, judge rate, prevent dilation, decompress"],"why":""},{"t":"Endoscopic hemostasis","tag":"","topic":"47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies","text":"EGD stops bleeding by <b>injecting chemicals</b>, applying <b>heat, electric current or laser</b>, or <b>banding or clipping</b> the vessel. Before: one or two <b>large-bore IVs</b> (moderate sedation, e.g., <b>midazolam</b> plus an opioid; transfusion), <b>NPO 4–6 hours</b>, signed <b>consent</b>. <b>After: VS, heart rhythm and SpO₂ until baseline; check swallowing — no food or fluid until the gag reflex returns</b> (the throat was numbed).","bullets":["EGD: <b>inject, heat/laser, band or clip</b>","Before: <b>large-bore IVs</b> (midazolam + opioid, blood), <b>NPO 4–6 h</b>, consent","After: VS, rhythm, SpO₂; <b>nothing by mouth until gag reflex returns</b>"],"why":""}]},{"k":"ta","gist":"<b>ABCs</b>: oxygen, <b>two large-bore IVs</b>, isotonic fluid and blood products; trend <b>VS, Hgb/Hct and SpO₂</b>; keep <b>NPO</b> and prepare for <b>EGD</b>.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Critical rescue","tag":"","topic":"47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies","text":"active upper GI bleeding is <b>life-threatening</b>: priority is <b>airway, breathing and circulation</b> — <b>oxygen</b> and ventilatory support as needed, <b>two large-bore IV lines</b> for fluids and blood, and monitoring of <b>vital signs, hematocrit and SpO₂</b>.","bullets":["<b>ABCs</b>: <b>oxygen</b>, <b>2 large-bore IVs</b>","Monitor <b>VS, Hct, SpO₂</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"If bleeding continues","tag":"","topic":"47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies","text":"<b>catheter-directed embolization</b> by interventional radiology (usually femoral access; an arteriogram finds the bleeder and material is injected to stop it) — care like any percutaneous vascular procedure. Then <b>aggressive acid suppression with PPIs</b> to <b>stabilize the clot</b> by raising gastric pH and prevent rebleeding; once bleeding stops, PPIs are also the main drug for <b>healing the ulcer</b>.","bullets":["<b>Embolization</b> (arteriogram, femoral) if scopes fail","<b>PPIs</b> raise pH → <b>stabilize the clot</b>; then <b>heal the ulcer</b>"],"why":""}]},{"k":"eo","gist":"<b>Hemodynamically stable</b>, <b>no orthostatic changes</b>, Hgb/Hct stable, no further bleeding; the client avoids NSAIDs, completes <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> therapy and can state when to seek care.","terms":[],"cards":[{"t":"Evaluate","tag":"","topic":"47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies","text":"no active PUD or <i>H. pylori</i>; pain relieved; the drugs are taken as prescribed (including the full <i>H. pylori</i> course) and the client avoids NSAIDs, alcohol, tobacco, bedtime snacks and the foods that cause pain; no upper GI bleeding — or bleeding controlled promptly.","bullets":["No PUD/<i>H. pylori</i>; pain controlled","Takes drugs (full <i>H. pylori</i> course); <b>no NSAIDs, alcohol, tobacco</b>, bedtime snacks","<b>No bleeding</b>"],"why":""},{"t":"Going home","tag":"","topic":"47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies","text":"discharge when there is <b>no ongoing bleeding, no orthostatic changes and no cardiopulmonary distress</b>. After major surgery or hemorrhage, one or two <b>home care visits</b>, especially for <b>older adults</b>. Home care (Box 47.3): VS with orthostatics, skin color, pain, <b>stool color and consistency</b>, bowel pattern, <b>Hgb/Hct</b>, bowel sounds; diet and <b>caffeine and alcohol</b>; understanding of drugs (<b>avoid NSAIDs</b>), smoking cessation. <b>Action alert — seek care at once</b> for <b>sudden, sharp, severe epigastric or abdominal pain</b>, <b>bloody or black stools</b>, or <b>bloody or coffee-ground vomit</b>.","bullets":["Discharge: <b>no bleeding, no orthostatics</b>, stable heart + lungs","After surgery/hemorrhage: <b>home care visits</b> (esp. <b>older adults</b>)","Home: orthostatics, <b>stool color</b>, Hgb/Hct, <b>no NSAIDs</b>, no smoking","Seek care: <b>sudden severe pain</b>, <b>black/bloody stool</b>, <b>coffee-ground vomit</b>"],"why":""}]}]}];
